  "narrative": "**Metabolomics Findings Narrative**\n\nThe differential abundance analysis identified eleven metabolites significantly altered between treatment and control groups. The most strongly enriched pathway, with a false-discovery rate (FDR) of 0.0 and a fold-enrichment of 603, is **Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism** (KEGG map00260), which encompassed seven of the eleven compounds. A secondary enriched network, supported by matching metabolites including phosphoserine, adenosine monophosphate, and S-adenosylmethionine, connects one-carbon and sulfur amino-acid metabolism with folate cycling.\n\nKey driver compounds within the primary pathway include **phosphocholine** (KEGG C00114), **phosphoserine** (KEGG C01005), and **S-adenosylmethionine** (SAM, KEGG C00019). All three are confirmed members of the glycine–serine–threonine axis and co-occur with the other measured metabolites with a pathway-sharing plausibility of 1.0.\n\nDirectional connectivity analysis verified the following relationships: phosphocholine sits upstream of SAM via a four-step KEGG route passing through phosphorylethanolamine and methionine intermediates, placing the choline-derived methyl donor downstream of membrane phospholipid metabolism. Phosphoserine is linked to sarcosine through a three-step path, indicating that one-carbon metabolism draws from serine-derived glycine pools.\n\nThe biological significance of these changes points to **disrupted one-carbon and methyl-group cycling**, which is essential for nucleotide synthesis, phospholipid biosynthesis, and epigenetic regulation. Elevated phosphocholine alongside altered SAM suggests perturbed phosphatidylcholine biosynthesis and transmethylation capacity. Concomitant shifts in cystathionine and lipoic acid indicate possible transsulfuration pathway activation, which may reflect altered redox homeostasis or oxidative-stress response in the treated system. The convergence of serine, choline, and methionine metabolism onto shared one-carbon pools provides a coherent mechanistic explanation for the observed differential abundances.",
